import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.Dkj3JE0_.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Ethics","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"blogs/CFA/C0 Ethics/Ethics.md","filePath":"blogs/CFA/C0 Ethics/Ethics.md"}'),r={name:"blogs/CFA/C0 Ethics/Ethics.md"};function n(s,e,l,d,c,u){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="ethics" tabindex="-1">Ethics <a class="header-anchor" href="#ethics" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Ethics&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="standard-i-professionalism" tabindex="-1">Standard I Professionalism <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-i-professionalism" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard I Professionalism&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="standard-i-a-knowledge-of-the-law" tabindex="-1">Standard I(A) Knowledge of the Law <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-i-a-knowledge-of-the-law" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard I(A) Knowledge of the Law&quot;">​</a></h3><blockquote><p>Members and Candidates must understand and comply with all applicable laws, rules, and regulations (including the CFA Institute Code of Ethics and Standards of Professional Conduct) of any government, regulatory organization, licensing agency, or professional association governing their professional activities. In the event of conflict, Members and Candidates must comply with the more strict law, rule, or regulation. Members and Candidates must not knowingly participate or assist in and must dissociate from any violation of such laws, rules, or regulations.</p></blockquote><p>Three duties packed into one Standard: <strong>know</strong>, <strong>comply with the stricter rule</strong>, and <strong>do not knowingly assist — dissociate</strong>.</p><h4 id="what-you-must-know-and-what-you-need-not" tabindex="-1">What you must know (and what you need not) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-you-must-know-and-what-you-need-not" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What you must know (and what you need not)&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>Understand the laws and regulations that <strong>directly govern</strong> your professional activities and that protect clients.</li><li>You are <strong>not</strong> required to become a compliance expert or to know every law that could conceivably touch your work.</li><li>Stay current when rules change — new products, new channels (social media, apps), and new local/regional/global requirements all count.</li><li>When unsure, use the firm’s compliance channels; seeking counsel is recommended, but <strong>reliance on counsel does not absolve</strong> you if the conduct still violates law or the Code and Standards.</li></ul><h4 id="stricter-of-applicable-law-vs-code-and-standards" tabindex="-1">Stricter of applicable law vs Code and Standards <a class="header-anchor" href="#stricter-of-applicable-law-vs-code-and-standards" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stricter of applicable law vs Code and Standards&quot;">​</a></h4><p>“Applicable law” is the law that governs the member’s or candidate’s conduct in the facts of the case. “More strict” means the rule that imposes <strong>greater restrictions</strong> or requires <strong>more protective action</strong> for investors.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Follow</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applicable law <strong>stricter</strong> than Code and Standards</td><td>Applicable law</td></tr><tr><td>Applicable law <strong>less strict</strong>, or silent / no securities law</td><td>Code and Standards</td></tr><tr><td>Conduct is legal under local law but violates the Code and Standards</td><td>Code and Standards (do not do it)</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="important custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">IMPORTANT</p><p>Applicable law is a <strong>floor</strong>, not a ceiling. If local law allows something the Code and Standards forbid (e.g. undisclosed referral fees), you still follow the Code and Standards.</p></div><p>Quick map for multi-jurisdiction cases (NS = no securities law; LS = less strict than Code/Standards; MS = more strict):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Where you operate / which law applies</th><th>Duty</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applicable law is LS or NS</td><td>Adhere to Code and Standards</td></tr><tr><td>Applicable law is MS</td><td>Adhere to that stricter law</td></tr><tr><td>Home law says “law of the locality where business is conducted governs,” and locality is LS/NS</td><td>Code and Standards</td></tr><tr><td>Home law says locality governs, and locality is MS</td><td>That stricter local law</td></tr><tr><td>Choice-of-law points to the <strong>client’s home country</strong></td><td>Whichever is stricter between that country’s law and the Code and Standards — usually: follow the stricter of the two</td></tr></tbody></table><h4 id="knowingly-participate-assist-—-and-dissociation" tabindex="-1">Knowingly participate / assist — and dissociation <a class="header-anchor" href="#knowingly-participate-assist-—-and-dissociation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Knowingly participate / assist — and dissociation&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>You are responsible for violations you <strong>knowingly</strong> participate in or assist. The Standard also reaches conduct you <strong>know or should know</strong> may contribute to a violation.</li><li>If you have reasonable grounds to believe client or employer activity is illegal or unethical: <strong>dissociate</strong>.</li><li>Escalation path when direct talk fails: <ol><li>Bring it to a supervisor or compliance.</li><li>Step away from the activity (remove your name from reports, refuse the assignment, stop using the misleading material, decline or drop the client).</li><li>In extreme cases, resign.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Inaction while staying associated</strong> can itself be treated as participation or assistance.</li><li>Reporting fellow members/candidates to CFA Institute is <strong>encouraged</strong>; reporting to regulators is required only when <strong>mandatory</strong> under applicable law (otherwise it is discretionary whistleblowing — get legal/compliance advice).</li></ul><div class="warning custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">WARNING</p><p>Using inflated composite performance in solicitations, continuing an underwriting after discovering a misleading prospectus, or staying silent on pay-for-flow disguised as “research” payments are classic assist/dissociate traps — even if you did not create the problem.</p></div><h4 id="cross-border-products-and-distribution" tabindex="-1">Cross-border products and distribution <a class="header-anchor" href="#cross-border-products-and-distribution" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cross-border products and distribution&quot;">​</a></h4><p>If you create or maintain products or packages, understand law in both <strong>origination</strong> and <strong>expected sale</strong> jurisdictions. Make reasonable efforts to check that distributors also respect distribution-country rules. When multi-jurisdiction complexity creates doubt, seek compliance or outside counsel.</p><h4 id="recommended-procedures-exam-useful-checklist" tabindex="-1">Recommended procedures (exam-useful checklist) <a class="header-anchor" href="#recommended-procedures-exam-useful-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recommended procedures (exam-useful checklist)&quot;">​</a></h4><p><strong>Individuals</strong></p><ul><li>Stay informed (memos, CLE / continuing education).</li><li>Review written compliance procedures regularly.</li><li>Keep current reference files of key statutes and rules.</li><li>Document dissociation steps; urge the firm to stop the conduct.</li><li>Know distribution-area laws for products you help build or sell.</li></ul><p><strong>Firms (encourage)</strong></p><ul><li>Adopt a code of ethics (e.g. Asset Manager Code as a template).</li><li>Centralize applicable-law information for staff.</li><li>Written protocols for reporting suspected violations.</li></ul><h4 id="exam-traps" tabindex="-1">Exam traps <a class="header-anchor" href="#exam-traps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Exam traps&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>“Legal counsel said it would be hard to prove” ≠ permission to ignore the violation.</li><li>Home-country charterholder status does <strong>not</strong> override a <strong>stricter</strong> host-country rule (e.g. local ban on personal IPO participation).</li><li>Minimal local insider-trading law does <strong>not</strong> relax Standard II(A) — follow the stricter Code/Standards rule.</li><li>New media (Facebook, X, IM) still need current regulatory compliance; ignorance of new guidance is still a Knowledge-of-the-Law failure.</li></ul><h3 id="standard-i-b-independence-and-objectivity" tabindex="-1">Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-i-b-independence-and-objectivity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity&quot;">​</a></h3><blockquote><p>Members and Candidates must use reasonable care and judgment to achieve and maintain independence and objectivity in their professional activities. Members and Candidates must not offer, solicit, or accept any gift, benefit, compensation, or consideration that reasonably could be expected to compromise their own or another’s independence and objectivity.</p></blockquote><p>Clients must get work and opinions <strong>unaffected by conflicts or pressure</strong>. Avoid both actual loss of independence/objectivity and situations <strong>perceived</strong> to cause that loss. Recommendations must state your <strong>true</strong> opinion in <strong>clear, unambiguous</strong> language — no soft-pedaling to please bankers, issuers, or sales.</p><h4 id="gifts-benefits-and-influence" tabindex="-1">Gifts, benefits, and influence <a class="header-anchor" href="#gifts-benefits-and-influence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Gifts, benefits, and influence&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Rule of thumb</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Third parties</strong> seeking influence (issuers, brokers, underwriters, rating sponsors, managers pitching for mandates)</td><td>Modest / token gifts and ordinary business entertainment may be OK; <strong>lavish</strong> gifts, entertainment, IPO allocations for your personal account, or anything that could reasonably compromise judgment → reject. Best practice: reject anything that could threaten independence.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Clients</strong> (already in a compensation relationship)</td><td>Gifts / “bonuses” are treated as <strong>supplementary compensation</strong>. Disclose to employer <strong>before</strong> acceptance when possible; otherwise disclose promptly after. Disclosure lets the firm monitor favoritism vs other clients.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="important custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">IMPORTANT</p><p><strong>Personal oversubscribed IPO allocations</strong> to managers are prohibited under I(B) — quick profits not available to clients.</p></div><p>Offering <strong>or</strong> soliciting compromising gifts violates I(B), including gifts that do not benefit you personally (e.g. donations to a charity or political campaign tied to a hiring decision). Both sides of manager selection / procurement are covered: do not buy influence; do not sell it.</p><h4 id="pressure-points-where-objectivity-breaks" tabindex="-1">Pressure points (where objectivity breaks) <a class="header-anchor" href="#pressure-points-where-objectivity-breaks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pressure points (where objectivity breaks)&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li><strong>Buy-side clients</strong> pressuring sell-side analysts against downgrades; retaliatory practices (reporting the analyst to the issuer) are improper.</li><li><strong>Investment banking</strong> pressure for favorable research on banking clients/prospects → firewalls, separate reporting lines, no IB veto over research, compensation <strong>not</strong> tied directly to banking deals; disclosures prominent and specific.</li><li><strong>Issuer / corporate access</strong> retaliation fears (cut off from calls/management) do not justify biased ratings.</li><li><strong>Credit rating agencies</strong> — fee and structuring conflicts; users of ratings should be mindful and may need to validate independently.</li><li><strong>Performance measurement</strong> — refuse requests to drop weak accounts from composites or to hide mandate drift.</li><li><strong>Issuer-paid research</strong> — allowed only with true independence, full conflict disclosure, diligence; compensation must be a <strong>flat fee not linked to conclusions</strong> (no success bonus, warrants, or equity kickers).</li><li><strong>Travel funding</strong> — best practice: commercial travel paid by you or your firm. Modest issuer-arranged travel only when commercial options are unavailable (e.g. remote property/mine tour). Sponsored “fact-finding” trips paid by a competing manager you later hire are a perception and conflict hazard.</li></ul><h4 id="recommended-procedures" tabindex="-1">Recommended procedures <a class="header-anchor" href="#recommended-procedures" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recommended procedures&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>Policy that research on corporate clients reflects the analyst’s <strong>unbiased</strong> opinion; compensation systems that protect research integrity.</li><li><strong>Restricted list</strong> if the firm will not allow adverse opinions — disseminate facts only.</li><li>Pay your own commercial transport and hotels for issuer visits; do not let one issuer always host.</li><li>Token gift limits (per gift and/or annual aggregate, calibrated to local custom); ordinary entertainment OK if not meant to influence.</li><li>Prior approval / disclosure for employee IPO participation; tight limits on private placements; supervise personal trading.</li><li>Written independence policy: research must not report to banking or other compromising departments.</li><li>Senior officer accountable for ethics/compliance; clear internal reporting channels.</li></ul><h4 id="decision-rules-for-vignettes" tabindex="-1">Decision rules for vignettes <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision-rules-for-vignettes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision rules for vignettes&quot;">​</a></h4><ol><li>Ask: could this gift, fee, trip, or bonus <strong>reasonably</strong> be expected to compromise my judgment — or look that way to a client?</li><li>Third-party inducement vs disclosed client gift — different treatment; disclosure is mandatory for client gifts.</li><li>Flat fee for issuer-paid work → possible; fee tied to “attracting investors” or positive tone → violation.</li><li>Intrafirm pressure (sales inventory, banking deal, boss’s prior “buy”) → still issue <strong>your</strong> independent conclusion, or refuse the constrained assignment; consider a restricted list.</li><li>Hiring / “pay-to-play” — political contributions or lavish entertainment aimed at winning mandates compromise the other party’s objectivity and violate I(B) for the offeror.</li></ol><div class="tip custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">TIP</p><p>Perception matters. Even when a modest business-only site visit paid by the issuer may not violate I(B), firm policies that always self-pay are safer. For manager due-diligence trips, your employer or the plan should pay base travel/lodging.</p></div><div class="warning custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">WARNING</p><p>Service-fee / trailer arrangements that lock you into pushing one product family after disclosure failures (or after underperformance, because you need the fee) are independence failures — often paired with VI(A) conflict disclosure issues.</p></div><h3 id="standard-i-c-misrepresentation" tabindex="-1">Standard I(C) Misrepresentation <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-i-c-misrepresentation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard I(C) Misrepresentation&quot;">​</a></h3><blockquote><p>Members and Candidates must not knowingly make any misrepresentations relating to investment analysis, recommendations, actions, or other professional activities.</p></blockquote><p>A <strong>misrepresentation</strong> is any untrue statement, omission of a fact, or other false or misleading statement. “Knowingly” covers what you know <strong>or should have known</strong> — including omissions that could alter an investment decision. Trust in the profession rests on accurate statements; false or misleading claims harm investors and market integrity.</p><h4 id="scope-what-you-must-not-misrepresent" tabindex="-1">Scope (what you must not misrepresent) <a class="header-anchor" href="#scope-what-you-must-not-misrepresent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scope (what you must not misrepresent)&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Rule of thumb</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Practice / credentials</strong></td><td>Qualifications, firm capabilities, services, performance record, investment characteristics — all must be accurate.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guarantees</strong></td><td>Do <strong>not</strong> guarantee a specific return or capital preservation on volatile investments. Built-in product guarantees (or institutional loss coverage) may be described accurately; explain limits (e.g. FDIC-insured CDs within insured amounts).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Third-party material</strong></td><td>Credit ratings, research, testimonials, marketing — if you use them in your business, <strong>you</strong> share responsibility for accuracy. Verify.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>External managers</strong></td><td>Disclose intended use; do <strong>not</strong> present their practices as your own.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Benchmarks / composites</strong></td><td>Use comparable benchmarks; disclose why a non-standard reference index is used. No cherry-picking accounts into composites. Do not “shop” illiquid valuations solely for higher marks.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Models / omissions</strong></td><td>Do not knowingly omit model inputs that mislead; do not present model output as fact.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Social media</strong></td><td>Same honesty rules as traditional client communications; perceived anonymity is not a shield.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Webpages</strong></td><td>Keep current; protect integrity/security; full disclosure, no misleading content.</td></tr></tbody></table><h4 id="plagiarism" tabindex="-1">Plagiarism <a class="header-anchor" href="#plagiarism" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plagiarism&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Copying or using others’ material in substantially the same form <strong>without acknowledgment</strong> violates I(C) — written <strong>and</strong> oral. Classic traps:</p><ul><li>Rebranding another firm’s report as your own</li><li>Near-verbatim excerpts without attribution</li><li>“Leading analysts say…” without naming sources</li><li>Using others’ forecasts/stats while dropping their caveats</li><li>Charts/graphs or proprietary models/spreadsheets without source/authorization</li><li>Combining undocumented ideas/forecasts to look original</li></ul><p><strong>OK:</strong> Firm may continue using research/models created by analysts who left (firm property); no need to attribute departed authors. You may <strong>not</strong> reissue an old report solely under your new personal name. Third-party research may be distributed if you do <strong>not</strong> claim authorship — disclose the source so clients know whose expertise they are buying.</p><h4 id="recommended-procedures-1" tabindex="-1">Recommended procedures <a class="header-anchor" href="#recommended-procedures-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recommended procedures&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>Know the firm’s real capabilities; use firm-approved service/qualification language; know who may speak for the firm.</li><li>Keep a personal qualification/experience summary.</li><li>Verify third-party capabilities before using their materials.</li><li>Monitor webpages for currency and security.</li><li><strong>Plagiarism hygiene:</strong> keep source copies; attribute quotations; attribute paraphrases/summaries.</li></ul><h4 id="exam-traps-1" tabindex="-1">Exam traps <a class="header-anchor" href="#exam-traps-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Exam traps&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>Unintentional typo discovered later → correct and stop distribution; <strong>not</strong> an I(C) violation if not knowing when made — but known false credentials used repeatedly → violation.</li><li>“Guaranteed by the government” for IO strips or similar when only the underlying mortgages (not the interest stream or principal economics) are guaranteed → classic misrepresentation.</li><li>Repackaging vendor research as “our work” → misrepresents extent of your work.</li><li>Citing only a newspaper that relayed a study → cite the <strong>actual</strong> source (or both if you rely on the intermediary’s interpretation).</li></ul><h4 id="application-cases" tabindex="-1">Application cases <a class="header-anchor" href="#application-cases" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Application cases&quot;">​</a></h4><p><strong>Example 4 — Plagiarism (Cindy Grant).</strong> Grant specializes in Canadian mining. She reads Barton’s extensive Jefferson Mining report, obtains new drilling data by phone, adds it to Barton’s analysis, and circulates the package as <strong>her</strong> report.<br><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Reproducing large parts of another analyst’s work without acknowledgment is plagiarism — even if you add a new data point.</p><p><strong>Example 5 — Misrepresenting an investment (Ricki Marks).</strong> Marks sells interest-only (IO) mortgage strips to public pension clients as “guaranteed by the US government.” Clients need government-guaranteed securities under policy/law. Only the underlying mortgages are guaranteed — not the IO interest stream or the investor’s economic outcome; rate declines and prepayments can destroy value.<br><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Accurate labels on complex products matter. Stretching “guaranteed” beyond what is actually guaranteed is an I(C) violation.</p><hr><h3 id="standard-i-d-misconduct" tabindex="-1">Standard I(D) Misconduct <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-i-d-misconduct" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard I(D) Misconduct&quot;">​</a></h3><blockquote><p>Members and Candidates must not engage in any professional conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, or deceit or commit any act that reflects adversely on their professional reputation, integrity, or competence.</p></blockquote><p>I(A) is about <strong>applicable law</strong> governing professional activity. I(D) is broader: any conduct that reflects poorly on <strong>professional</strong> integrity, reputation, or competence — lying, cheating, stealing, or other dishonest acts that adversely affect professional activities. The Code and Standards primarily target <strong>professional-life</strong> conduct (CFA Institute discourages unethical personal behavior generally, but I(D) is not a catch-all for every private misdeed).</p><h4 id="what-counts-and-what-usually-does-not" tabindex="-1">What counts (and what usually does not) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-counts-and-what-usually-does-not" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What counts (and what usually does not)&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Conduct</th><th>I(D)?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dishonesty, fraud, deceit tied to work or that damages professional trustworthiness</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Behavior (even if not illegal) that impairs ability to perform duties (e.g. intoxication while making investment decisions)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Skipping due diligence and relying on others so clients’ trust is abused</td><td>Can be (absence of appropriate effort)</td></tr><tr><td>Personal bankruptcy <strong>without</strong> fraudulent/deceitful business conduct</td><td>Generally no</td></tr><tr><td>Civil disobedience / protest arrests that do not reflect on professional reputation, integrity, or competence</td><td>Generally no</td></tr><tr><td>Using CFA Institute enforcement to settle personal/political scores unrelated to professional ethics</td><td>Misuse; not a proper I(D) path</td></tr></tbody></table><h4 id="recommended-procedures-firms-—-encourage" tabindex="-1">Recommended procedures (firms — encourage) <a class="header-anchor" href="#recommended-procedures-firms-—-encourage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recommended procedures (firms — encourage)&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>Firm code of ethics: personal behavior that reflects poorly on the individual, firm, or industry will not be tolerated.</li><li>List of potential violations and sanctions (up to dismissal).</li><li>Reference checks so hires are of good character and not barred from the industry.</li></ul><h4 id="exam-traps-2" tabindex="-1">Exam traps <a class="header-anchor" href="#exam-traps-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Exam traps&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>Workplace fraud (e.g. falsified insurance claims) → I(D) even if “not about securities.”</li><li>Charity/volunteer role used for kickbacks → still fraud/deceit under I(D).</li><li>Seeing likely misreporting and being told to “stay away” → escalate with proof per firm policy; consider regulator if internal channels fail (also IV(A)/IV(C)).</li></ul><h4 id="application-cases-1" tabindex="-1">Application cases <a class="header-anchor" href="#application-cases-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Application cases&quot;">​</a></h4><p><strong>Example 1 — Professionalism and competence (Simon Sasserman).</strong> A trust officer lunches daily with heavy drinking; clients see it; after lunch he is intoxicated while making investment decisions. Colleagues only deal with him in the morning.<br><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Alcohol abuse that impairs professional judgment and raises competence doubts violates I(D) — it reflects poorly on him, the employer, and the industry.</p><p><strong>Example 4 — Personal actions and integrity (Carmen Garcia).</strong> An SRI fund manager / environmental activist is repeatedly arrested for nonviolent trespass at a petrochemical plant.<br><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Civil disobedience tied to personal beliefs generally <strong>does not</strong> violate I(D) when it does not reflect poorly on professional reputation, integrity, or competence.</p><hr><h3 id="standard-i-e-competence" tabindex="-1">Standard I(E) Competence <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-i-e-competence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard I(E) Competence&quot;">​</a></h3><blockquote><p>Members and Candidates must act with and maintain the competence necessary to fulfill their professional responsibilities.</p></blockquote><p>Supports the Code duties to act with competence and diligence and to <strong>maintain and improve</strong> professional competence. Competence = sufficient <strong>knowledge, skills, and abilities</strong> for <strong>that specific role</strong> — not a single exam score or degree. What “competent” looks like depends on facts and duties; roles change, so the duty is to <strong>achieve and maintain</strong> competence over time.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Knowledge</strong></td><td>Body of information applied to the function — and how effectively it is applied</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Skills</strong></td><td>Role-specific capabilities to complete tasks and goals</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Abilities</strong></td><td>Capabilities and attitudes that produce observable professional outcomes</td></tr></tbody></table><h4 id="key-rules-of-thumb" tabindex="-1">Key rules of thumb <a class="header-anchor" href="#key-rules-of-thumb" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Key rules of thumb&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li><strong>Bad outcome ≠ incompetence.</strong> A competent manager can lose money; a competent analyst can miss a forecast after thorough work. Hidden fraud that misled the whole market may not make a diligent recommender “incompetent.”</li><li><strong>Education ≠ competence for a new domain.</strong> A strong auditor may not be ready for GIPS compliance reviews; a general consultant may lack skills for a sustainable-manager search.</li><li><strong>License/pass ≠ enough.</strong> Passing a sales licensing exam without learning the fairness rules you will apply still fails I(E).</li><li><strong>New role / new product:</strong> get up to speed <strong>before</strong> acting (supervision skills, ESG, crypto, etc.). Do not invest client money in products you do not understand to chase a trend or retain clients.</li><li><strong>CE is expected in spirit, not a mandated program.</strong> Conferences, certifications, employer training, self-study, expert groups — any path that keeps you proficient.</li></ul><h4 id="recommended-procedures-2" tabindex="-1">Recommended procedures <a class="header-anchor" href="#recommended-procedures-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recommended procedures&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>Ongoing professional development / CE; certifications where useful.</li><li>Conferences, seminars, webinars; employer training; disciplined self-study.</li><li>Expert groups; deliberately build new skills when responsibilities change.</li></ul><h4 id="exam-traps-3" tabindex="-1">Exam traps <a class="header-anchor" href="#exam-traps-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Exam traps&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>Promoting someone into research director / supervisor without building <strong>management and compliance</strong> competence for the new seat.</li><li>Adding ESG (or any new lens) to reports <strong>after</strong> hiring expertise and training the team → compliant path; bolting it on with no knowledge → not.</li><li>Rushing into unfamiliar products (e.g. crypto) on social-media hype without understanding nature or suitability → I(E) violation (often also suitability / diligence issues).</li></ul><h4 id="application-cases-2" tabindex="-1">Application cases <a class="header-anchor" href="#application-cases-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Application cases&quot;">​</a></h4><p><strong>Example 6 — Understanding new investment products (Alex Halsey).</strong> Clients push for cryptocurrency after seeing fast gains. Halsey has only general newspaper awareness, does not understand the asset or product differences, but buys a heavily promoted crypto product for clients rather than look out of touch or lose business.<br><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Limited knowledge + hasty client investments in unfamiliar products = failure to act with required competence.</p><p><strong>Example 5 — Choosing investments (Kwame Mifune).</strong> After thorough research (credit, financials, track record), Mifune recommends Vouchsafe life policies for suitable clients. Unknown CFO fraud later bankrupts the insurer; policies become worthless. Clients call him incompetent.<br><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Thorough, appropriate research that still misses industry-wide deception does <strong>not</strong>, by itself, prove incompetence under I(E). Outcome alone is not the test.</p><hr><h3 id="standard-ii-a-material-nonpublic-information" tabindex="-1">Standard II(A) Material Nonpublic Information <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-ii-a-material-nonpublic-information" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard II(A) Material Nonpublic Information&quot;">​</a></h3><blockquote><p>Members and Candidates who possess material nonpublic information that could affect the value of an investment must not act or cause others to act on the information.</p></blockquote><p>Trading — or inducing others to trade — on material nonpublic information (MNPI) erodes market confidence. The ban covers equities <strong>and</strong> derivatives, funds, and other alternatives. Selectively “knowing” the market is perceived as rigged harms capital formation for everyone.</p><h4 id="material-vs-nonpublic" tabindex="-1">Material vs nonpublic <a class="header-anchor" href="#material-vs-nonpublic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Material vs nonpublic&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Material</strong></td><td>Disclosure would probably affect price, <strong>or</strong> a reasonable investor would want it before deciding — i.e. it would significantly alter the total mix of available information. Factors: specificity, divergence from public info, nature, <strong>reliability of source</strong>, clarity of price impact, passage of time.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nonpublic</strong></td><td>Not yet disseminated / available to the market generally. Waiting for the slowest reader is unnecessary once truly disseminated — but a room of analysts or a small shareholder meeting is <strong>not</strong> public dissemination.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Often material:</strong> earnings; M&amp;A / tender / JV; asset quality shifts; major product/patent/regulatory outcomes; big customer/supplier changes; management or auditor changes; security events (defaults, splits, dividends, offerings); bankruptcy; significant litigation; market-moving government data; large unexecuted orders; third-party rating/recommendation changes.</p><p><strong>Reliability matters:</strong> a corporate insider’s fact about a major contract → likely material; a competitor’s speculation → often not. Drug-trial facts from people running the trial → likely material; educated conjecture from unconnected experts → usually not.</p><p><strong>Selective disclosure:</strong> company tips to a few analysts/investors stay nonpublic until generally available. Guidance that “interprets” filings for a favored group can also be MNPI. Legitimate due-diligence MNPI under a deal agreement (merger, underwriting, rating, offering) may be used <strong>for that engagement</strong> — not for trading or tipping.</p><h4 id="mosaic-theory-exam-emphasis" tabindex="-1">Mosaic theory (exam emphasis) <a class="header-anchor" href="#mosaic-theory-exam-emphasis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Mosaic theory (exam emphasis)&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Under the <strong>mosaic theory</strong>, an analyst may base recommendations on conclusions drawn from:</p><ol><li><strong>Public</strong> information, plus</li><li><strong>Nonmaterial nonpublic</strong> information</li></ol><p>…even if the <strong>conclusion itself</strong> would have been material inside information had a company insider whispered it whole. Piecing together a mosaic of public facts and small, nonmaterial private scraps (customer/supplier chatter, designer/retailer opinions, labor color, FX views, etc.) is core analytical work and <strong>does not</strong> violate II(A).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Mosaic OK</th><th>Violation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Public filings + plant visit observations + nonmaterial color from customers/competitors → sell rating anticipating weak earnings</td><td>Acting on a specific, reliable insider tip (pending deal, next week’s strike, trial failure) before public release</td></tr><tr><td>Documented interviews and analysis supporting a rating change</td><td>Tipping others (including family) with MNPI, or trading ahead of a market-moving <strong>unpublished</strong> analyst recommendation you received early</td></tr><tr><td>Expert-network call that deepens industry understanding <strong>without</strong> confidential trial/company results</td><td>Expert (or anyone) gives material confidential results → no trading until public; you must judge materiality yourself — signed “no MNPI” forms do not absolve you</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Document the mosaic.</strong> Save research supporting that the conclusion came from public + nonmaterial nonpublic sources [see also V(C) Record Retention]. Documentation is your evidence if someone later claims you used MNPI.</p><p><strong>Analyst reports as MNPI for others:</strong> A star analyst’s unpublished recommendation can be material to the market. The analyst may give it to <strong>clients</strong> (paid work under mosaic/public inputs) without making it free to the world. Recipients who are <strong>not</strong> clients and get it early (e.g. a TV producer) must not trade or tip.</p><p><strong>Social media:</strong> Membership-gated groups are not automatically “public.” Confirm material items via true public channels (filings, press, open webpages) before treating them as public. Client/public-wide posts are fine if regulatory filings and I(A) duties are met.</p><h4 id="recommended-procedures-3" tabindex="-1">Recommended procedures <a class="header-anchor" href="#recommended-procedures-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recommended procedures&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>If you get MNPI: push for <strong>public dissemination</strong>; if not possible, tell only designated compliance/supervisors — <strong>no</strong> trading or recommendation changes; do not coax insiders into private tips.</li><li>Firm compliance: trade review, recommendation review, documented procedures, interdepartment supervision.</li><li>Equitable disclosure culture (small vs large brokers; buy-side vs sell-side; no blackballing negative analysts).</li><li><strong>Firewalls:</strong> control interdepartment flow (preferably via compliance/legal clearance); watch / restricted / rumor lists; document and enforce; heighten review of proprietary trading when MNPI is held; physical separation and no personnel overlap across the wall; “over the wall” only via compliance; one authority decides materiality/public status.</li><li>Personal trading limits; restricted vs watch lists (broad restricted lists can tip the market).</li><li>Market-making while holding MNPI: stay <strong>passive</strong> (contra to unsolicited customer flow). Risk-arbitrage: strongest case to <strong>suspend</strong> when on watch list.</li><li>Train all employees to recognize MNPI and escalate.</li></ul><h4 id="exam-traps-4" tabindex="-1">Exam traps <a class="header-anchor" href="#exam-traps-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Exam traps&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li>Tip chains: telling a broker who buys → both tipper and trader can violate.</li><li>Overheard IB earnings drop with salespeople walking through → failed barrier; traders on that info violate; the person who let it leak can violate too.</li><li>Unreliable gossip (doctor’s casual takeover chat) → often <strong>not</strong> material → trading after other diligence may be OK.</li><li>“My friend is a senior exec and wouldn’t break the law” + sweet earnings whisper → still your duty to avoid trading on MNPI; profits or losses irrelevant.</li><li>Expert networks: allowed for education; <strong>not</strong> for confidential trial/product results used to trade.</li></ul><h4 id="application-cases-3" tabindex="-1">Application cases <a class="header-anchor" href="#application-cases-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Application cases&quot;">​</a></h4><p><strong>Example 5 — Applying the mosaic theory (Jagdish Teja).</strong> Buy-side furniture analyst studies financials, visits operations, and talks to designers and retailers about which styles sell. No clear buy appears, but Swan Furniture’s costly new designs are out of favor while peers sell conservative lines. From that mix plus P&amp;L work, Teja concludes next-quarter earnings will drop sharply and issues a sell; managers cut positions.<br><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Quarterly earnings figures are material and nonpublic — but Teja’s <strong>conclusion</strong>, reached from public information plus <strong>nonmaterial</strong> nonpublic color, is classic mosaic and <strong>not</strong> a II(A) violation. Document those sources.</p><p><strong>Example 3 — Selective disclosure (Elizabeth Levenson).</strong> Among the 10 largest shareholders invited to meet a manufacturer’s finance director, she learns the workforce will strike next Friday and cripple operations. She wants to cut the rating from buy to sell.<br><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Material information shared in a small group is still <strong>nonpublic</strong>. Until the company disseminates it to the market generally, she must not act on it.</p>',107)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",n]]);export{h as __pageData,m as default};
